## Forms

- React Hook Form
- Zod validation

Display field-level validation errors on the field helpers.

### Form messages (content footer)

All submit / API success and error messages must use `useFormMessage()` (`showSuccess`, `showError`, `showInfo`, `showErrors`). Do not render ad-hoc success/error banners inside forms or pages.

- Inside the authenticated shell, messages appear in the content section footer (`FormMessageInline` in `AppLayout`).
- Outside the shell (e.g. login), the same API shows floating toasts.
- Keep field validation errors on the input; put operational messages in the footer.
- Prefer `useFormMessage` from `@/hooks/useFormMessage` or `@/components/forms`.

### Autofill

Browser autofill must always stay disabled on every form. Never enable it unless explicitly requested.

- Every form must set `autoComplete="off"` (the `FormPanel` wrapper does this).
- Field helpers (`FormInput`, `FormSelect`, `FormTextarea`, `FormAutocomplete`) default to `autoComplete="new-password"` because Chrome ignores `"off"` on address/email/password fields. They also set `data-1p-ignore` and `data-lpignore` to suppress password managers.
- Any raw `input`, `select`, or `textarea` must apply the same attributes (`autoComplete="new-password"`, `data-1p-ignore`, `data-lpignore`).
- Spread `{...props}` before these attributes so callers cannot re-enable autofill by accident.
- Prefer `type="text"` with an appropriate `inputMode` over `type="email"` / `type="tel"` unless a native type is explicitly required.
- `FormInput` must remain read-only until the user focuses it, then lock again on blur. This prevents Chrome from injecting credentials before user interaction.
- Recognizable login fields must use `autoComplete="one-time-code"` in addition to the read-only focus gate because Chrome may ignore both `"off"` and `"new-password"`.
- Apply `aria-autocomplete="none"`, `data-bwignore`, `data-protonpass-ignore`, and `data-form-type="other"` where browser or password-manager autofill is likely.

